        <h2 class="subhead" >Info</h1>
            This repo implements a RISC-V processor, specifically the unpriviledged RV32I Base integer instruction set. 
            The core was tested on a Atrix-7 FPGA.
            The repo also includes a testing environment to compile + run test programs, and compare ouptuts against a RISC ISA emulator 
            and against x86 execution for logical results.

            This project is still in progress. Below are current features of this repo:
            <ul>
                <li>6-Stage pipline implementing RV32I ISA (F-D-X-M1-M2-W)</li>
                <li>Hazard detection but no forwarding yet</li>
                <li>RISC emulator. To generate expected memory ouput, and for the future for expected memory traces for a given program. (DCCM reads/writes will be consistant using this architecture) </li>
                <li>1 Instruction + 1 Data Local Memory Bus Port (LMB). Each requires 1 cycle read/write, </li>
                <li>32KB ICCM, 32KB DCCM, both implemented using BRAM blocks. </li>
                <li>Memoy mapped GPIO. Allowes for access to LEDs/Switches.</li>
                <li>2 verification environments for multi-level verification. (1) cpu level to verifiy pipeline execution. (2) fpga/system level to verify memory + other external devices.</li>
                <li>Timing closed at 100 MHz.</li>
            </ul>

        <h2 class="subhead" id="mini-regex">Usage</h1>
            <p>
                Setting up the repo:
<pre><code class="language-bash">git clone https://github.com/yiannis-cunning/processor
cd processor
# Fix paths in setenvs.sh to point to vivado installation
source setenvs.sh
</code></pre>

                RISC-V Simulator
                <pre><code class="language-bash">./cpu/dv/risk_emulator/build/emulator memory_image.ihex PC_start_addr -o mem_dump_loc -n mem_dump_size</code></pre>
                Compiling C test programs:
                <ul>
                    <li>Given some hardware starting PC address, desired stack address, ICCM/DCCM addresses </li>
                    <li>Each .c test is compiled for the local computer architecture, and for RISC, RV32I arch.</li>
                    <li>The test is run on the local machine to capture the 'good' results (DCCM dump) </li>
                    <li>.ihex is dumped for the RISC executable. The emulator is run using this and the DCCM from this is also dumped as reference.</li>
                    <li>hexdumps are saved of the RISC executable so it can be loaded in to SRAM banks in vivado simulations</li>
                </ul>
                <pre><code class="language-bash">python cpu/dv/scripts/compile_c_tests.py</code></pre>
                Running CPU level tests:
                <pre><code class="language-bash">python cpu/dv/scripts/compile_design.py --tests "main add jump" --out_dir ./ </code></pre>
                Create Vivado project: (In current directory)
                <pre><code class="language-bash">source fpga/design/make_ws.sh</code></pre>
                Compile FPGA test programs/FW:
                <pre><code class="language-bash">python fpga/dv/c_src/compile.py</code></pre>
                Running FPGA level tests:
                <pre><code class="language-bash">python cpu/dv/scripts/compile_design.py --tests "main add jump" --out_dir ./ </code></pre>
            

            </p>




